@startuml
title General Authorisation Model

'#############################################
'#              Classes                      #
'#############################################

class AssignmentRule {
 +name: string
 +operator: [AND, OR, NOT, NULL]
 +hasSubrule:
 +authorisee:
 +jointType: ENUM [Alone, All, Majority,
 At Least x of y]
 +requiredNumberOfAuthorisees: Integer
 +totalNumberOfGranteesInRule: Integer
}

class "<i>AssignmentRule (Alias)</i>" as AssignmentRule_

note right of AssignmentRule
 OR is inclusive: at least one alternative subrule
 must be satisfied. Satisfying additional alternatives
 is permitted but is not required.

 Compound rules combine subrules using operator.
 Rules listing authorisees use jointType and the total y.
 These participation fields do not apply to compound rules.

 Alone: at least one eligible actor is sufficient;
 additional participation is permitted.
 All: all y eligible actors must participate.
 Majority: more than half of y must participate.
 At Least x of y: at least x eligible actors must participate.
 requiredNumberOfAuthorisees supplies x for this threshold.

 totalNumberOfGranteesInRule supplies y: the total number
 of distinct eligible actors, not involvement entries.
 A role-based involvement may identify multiple actors.
 An actor holding multiple qualifying roles counts once
 within the rule. Count eligibility at the time of the action.
end note

class Authorisation {
 +dct:identifier: uri
 *dct:title: Literal
 *entityConcerned:
 *dct:temporal:
 *hasLegalBasisIn:
 *authorisationRule:
 +hasSupportingInvolvementOf:
 +status: ENUM [active, inactive]
 *dateOfIssue: date
 +type: Skos Concept
 +replaces:
 +delegable: [Y/N]
 *hasRule:
 +isDocumentedIn: uri
 +hasScope:
}

class "<i>Authorisation (Alias)</i>" as Authorisation_

class AuthorisationRule {
 +name: string
 +operator: [AND, OR, NOT, NULL]
 hasSubrule:
 *authoriser:
}

class "<i>AuthorisationRule (Alias)</i>" as AuthorisationRule_

class Authority {

}

class By'Law {
 *hasApplicableLaw:
}

class CodeListReference {
 *listIdentifier: string
 *listVersion: string
 *entryCode: string
 +label: string
 +uri: uri
}

note right of CodeListReference
 Every code-list reference must identify the managed list,
 its specific version, and the entry code.
 The list identifier must distinguish its managing
 organisation or publisher. A public URI is optional.

 Interpretation and descendant traversal use the specified
 version. Later list versions do not automatically change
 the scope of an existing authorisation.
 This requirement also applies to code-list references
 used for units or other coded fields.

 The same reference may be reused wherever the same list,
 version and entry are intended. label is optional display
 text; it does not replace entryCode or identify the entry.
 A symbol or place name must not be assumed to be its code.

 The displayed code-list boxes are illustrative sources,
 not separate property types or mandatory dependencies.
 Codes and hierarchies are resolved using the identified
 managed list and version; a reference selects one entry.
end note

class  EconomicOperator {
 *legalName: string
 +registryNumber: string
 *EUID: identifier
 pattern: "^[A-Z]{2}[A-Z]{3}\\.
 [a-zA-Z0-9]+\\.[a-zA-Z0-9]+$"
}

class "<i>EconomicOperator (Alias)</i>" as EconomicOperator_

class IdentificationDocument {
 *number: string
 *name: string
 +type: CodeListReference
}

class Involvement {
 +code: string
 *name: string
 *dct:temporal:
 +member:
 +hasRole:
 *inOrganisation:
}

class "<i>Involvement (Alias1)</i>" as Involvement_

class "<i>Involvement (Alias2)</i>" as Involvement__

note right of Involvement
 A member may be specified to identify a particular actor.
 If member is absent, hasRole and inOrganisation
 must both be supplied. The involvement then designates
 any eligible holder of that role in that organisation.
 When authority is exercised, the actual actor must
 hold the specified role at the time of the action.
end note

class Jurisdiction {
 
}

class Law {
 *hasJurisdiction: 
 *code: string
 *name: string
 *authority:
}

class LegalBasis {
' Legal Basis is a Role that the law or an other
' authorisation gan give.
 +hasApplicableLaw:
 +hasApplicableAuthorisation:
 +isInfluencedBy:
}

class "<i>LegalBasis (Alias)</i>" as LegalBasis_

note right of LegalBasis
 A role played by a law, bylaw, another authorisation,
 or another qualifying source when it provides the
 legal grounding for a particular authorisation.
 An authorisation may have multiple legal bases.
 Every authorisation must reference at least one legal basis.

 Each LegalBasis must reference at least one applicable
 law or applicable authorisation, or both. Each source
 property is individually optional (0..n), but their
 combined number of source references must be at least one.
 Bylaws are covered by the existing subclass of Law.
 isInfluencedBy does not replace the required source.
end note

class Machine {
 *id: url
 +name: string
}

class "<i>Machine (Alias)</i>" as Machine_

class Operator {
 +name: string
 *operation: [AND,OR, XOR, NULL]
' +hasSuboperator:
 +hasRule:
}

class PeriodOfTime {
 +startDate: date
 +endDate: date
}

class Person {
 *nameFirst: string
 *nameLast: string
 +title: string
 +dateOfBirth: date
 +placeOfBirth: CodeListReference
 +fullAdress:
 +identificationDocument:
}

Class "<i>Person (Alias)</i>" as Person_

class Presidence {
 +name:
 *description: string
 *hasJurisdiction:
 *influences:
}

class PrivateEvaluationDefinition {
 *identifier: uri
 *serviceEndpoint: uri
 *checkDefinition: uri
 *evaluatedBy:
}

class PrivateRestrictionResult {
 *restriction:
 *transactionIdentifier: uri
 *evaluationDefinition:
 *outcome: ENUM [satisfied, not satisfied, undetermined]
}

note right of PrivateEvaluationDefinition
 An atomic restriction has exactly one evaluationMode.
 Local: inputDefinition is required and identifies a
 machine-resolvable definition of the transaction input
 or calculation. privateEvaluationDefinition is absent.
 The relying party compares the input with value using
 the restriction's operator and unit where applicable.

 Private: privateEvaluationDefinition is required;
 inputDefinition is absent. The definition identifies
 the Economic Operator, service endpoint and defined check.
 The service receives the requested transaction and the
 applicable restriction, evaluates the complete comparison
 within the Economic Operator's environment, and returns
 its result rather than the confidential calculated input.

 Compound restrictions have none of these evaluation fields;
 they combine the results of their child restrictions.
end note

note right of PrivateRestrictionResult
 A result belongs to exactly one restriction, requested
 transaction and private evaluation definition.
 The evaluation definition must match the one referenced
 by that restriction, whose mode must be Private.
 The result records the outcome of that restriction only;
 it does not represent the relying party's final acceptance.
 Confidential input values and intermediate totals are
 not included. The eWallet environment handles trust.
end note

class Restriction {
 +evaluationMode: ENUM [Local, Private]
 +inputDefinition: uri
 +privateEvaluationDefinition:
 *operator: [!=, <, <=, =, >=, >, AND, OR, NOT]
 +value: [Literal or CodeListReference]
 +unit: [Unit or CodeListReference]
 +hasSubrestriction:
 }
 
class "<i>Restriction (Alias)</i>" as Restriction_

note right of Restriction
 A restriction must be referenced by at least one
 parent restriction or directly by at least one Scope.
 Both kinds of reference are permitted.

 Every restriction must be reachable from a Scope
 through zero or more hasSubrestriction links.
 Cycles are not permitted.

 A restriction may have zero to n recorded children.
 No recorded children does not, by itself, establish
 that none exist.

 A restriction may be reused by multiple parents
 or scopes. Changes affect every referencing context.
 To change only one use, create a separate restriction
 and replace the reference for that use.

 A comparison value may be a literal or a reference
 to a concept in a managed code list.
 unit is optional and identifies a measurement unit
 or currency when applicable.

 For numbers or quantities, input <= value means
 numerically less than or equal, using compatible units.
 For code-list concepts, input <= value means the input
 is the selected concept or a descendant at any depth.
 Use the specified managed hierarchy, not code-prefix
 or numerical comparison of the codes.
 = and != compare exact values or exact concepts;
 != does not exclude a concept's descendants.
 Use NOT around a <= hierarchy check to exclude a subtree.
 <, > and >= apply to comparable ordered literal values;
 their meaning for concept hierarchies is not defined here.

 Atomic restrictions use a comparison operator, require
 value, and have no children. unit is used when applicable.
 Compound restrictions have no comparison value or unit.
 AND has two or more children; all must be satisfied.
 OR has two or more children; at least one must be satisfied.
 Additional satisfied OR alternatives are permitted.
 NOT has exactly one child and reverses its result.
 To exclude each of several alternatives, use NOT(OR(...)).
 To exclude only their conjunction, use NOT(AND(...)).

 Evaluation results are satisfied, not satisfied, or
 undetermined. NOT of undetermined remains undetermined.
 AND is not satisfied if any child is not satisfied;
 it is satisfied if all are satisfied, otherwise undetermined.
 OR is satisfied if any child is satisfied;
 it is not satisfied if all are not satisfied,
 otherwise undetermined.
 A missing operand or incomplete child set must not be
 treated as a complete successful evaluation.
 A scope passes only when its overall result is satisfied.

 Code lists may come from standards or be maintained
 by an organisation. A public URI is not required;
 the reference must identify the list, its mandatory
 version, and its entry using CodeListReference.
end note

class Role {
 *name:
 *inOrganisation:
}

class "<i>Role (Alias)</i>" as Role_

class "<i>Role (upper ontology)</i>" as _Role

class Rule {
 *isApplicableWhithin:
 *isDerivedFrom:
 +hasPart:
}

class "<i>Rule (Alias)</i>" as Rule_

note right of Rule
 A rule that implements or conforms to an applicable
 law or bylaw, including a business rule.
 It is derived from one or more legal bases
 and may be composed of subrules.
end note

class Scope {
 *title: string
 +hasRestriction:[operator in subproperty]
}

note right of Scope
 The eWallet environment provides the trust framework.
 Scope evaluation determines whether the requested
 process, service or contract falls within the authorisation.

 Online services are normally specified by an eService
 restriction, not by a geographical restriction.
 The explicit restriction expression determines which
 checks apply and how they combine. There is no implicit
 geographical check or default combination with eService.
 Where a geographical restriction is explicitly specified
 for online business, the input is the site's market.
 Each restriction identifies its input and authorised
 concept; code-list references include the list version.

 The relying party evaluates restrictions using the
 transaction data and the specified code-list versions.
 Restrictions requiring private data are evaluated within
 the Economic Operator's environment. Only the result
 for the relevant restriction and transaction is presented;
 the underlying private values are not disclosed.

 The relying party combines its own checks with the
 private restriction results and makes the final decision
 to accept the authority for the requested transaction.
end note

class Unit {
 *exponent: RestrictionValue
 *unit: CodeListReference
 }

note right of Unit
 Unit represents a unit term with an exponent; its base
 unit is identified by a versioned CodeListReference.
 A coded unit or currency can be referenced directly by
 Restriction.unit. Compound units can use Unit terms.
 These are alternative representations, not cumulative
 requirements. A unit is absent where not applicable.
end note

class Entity {
 *mandatory
 +optional
}

